            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to show, then the Latin for to make known."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: aberrō, aberrāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "aberrō, aberrāre — \"to stray, to wander from the way\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In nebulā viātōrēs ā viā aberrant. In the fog the travellers stray from the road."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "aberrō, aberrāre, aberrāvī, aberrātum, with ā or ab. English aberration comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "aberrō is built from ab 'away, from' and errō 'to wander, to go astray' (EtymDB; Lewis & Short). English aberration comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
